# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true is set on at least one developer machine, so without this
# file a fresh clone on Windows gets CRLF in the working copy. That is harmless
# for most files and fatal for a few:
#
#   user_data.sh.tftpl  Terraform reads it from the working copy and embeds it
#                       in EC2 user data. A CRLF shebang -- "#!/bin/bash\r" --
#                       makes the kernel look for an interpreter named
#                       "/bin/bash\r", which does not exist. The instance boots
#                       with no engine and no proxy, and the only evidence is an
#                       empty bootstrap log.
#   Makefile            recipe lines are tab-significant; make on Linux does not
#                       accept a trailing \r.
#   *.sh                same shebang problem, everywhere.
#
# `* text=auto` normalises to LF in the repository regardless of platform; the
# explicit `eol=lf` entries additionally force LF in the WORKING COPY, which is
# what Terraform and make actually read.

* text=auto

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.tftpl     text eol=lf
*.sql       text eol=lf
*.py        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.tmpl      text eol=lf
# Everything under deploy/ is copied verbatim onto a Linux host: a systemd unit
# with CRLF reads "" as part of every value, and fails in ways that name the
# value rather than the line ending.
deploy/**   text eol=lf
*.mk        text eol=lf

# Windows-native files keep CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf
*.bat       text eol=crlf
*.cmd       text eol=crlf

# Reference-data snapshots are left byte-for-byte as committed. They are dated
# captures of what a source said on a given day, and renormalising them would
# rewrite evidence to fix a whitespace preference.
*.csv       -text

# Never touched.
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
*.zip       binary
